CONTACT;X-BEDEWORK-UID=18832edc-1b4f7314-011b-9d0edffd-0000097c:Okazaki\, 
 Hank
X-BEDEWORK-COST:Free and Open to the Public
CREATED:20250901T191254Z
DESCRIPTION:New 4K restoration celebrating the 40th anniversary of John Wo
 o's landmark action classic!\n\n"A Better Tomorrow"\n(John Woo\, 1986\, 9
 5 min\, Hong Kong\, Cantonese with English subtitles\, DCP)\n\nA reformin
 g ex-gangster tries to reconcile with his estranged policeman brother\, b
 ut the ties to his former gang are difficult to break.\n\nThe box-office 
 sensation that established director John Woo as the king of 1980s Hong Ko
 ng action cinema and set the standard for the heroic bloodshed genre\, th
 is electrifying gangster saga combines the director's intricate bullet pl
 ay with operatic emotion as it tracks the explosive relationship between 
 two brothers\, one a criminal trying to reform (Ti Lung)\, the other a fl
 edgling graduate of the HK police academy (Leslie Cheung) who blames his 
 brother for their father's death. The plot revolves around the split when
  the younger brother learns the other is a criminal and the efforts of th
 e criminal brother to reform. Along the way are plenty of heists\, double
 -crosses\, and shoot outs\, and in the first of his celebrated collaborat
 ions with Woo\, Chow Yun-fat radiates his matchless cool as a hit man who
  lives by his own code.\n\n"After an up and down decade as a director for
  hire\, working alternately in the wuxia and wacky comedy genres\, John W
 oo finally hit it big in 1986 when he teamed up with Tsui Hark and the Ci
 nema City studio to remake Patrick Lung Kong's 1967 drama THE STORY OF A 
 DISCHARGED PRISONER. One of the most influential films of the past 30 yea
 rs\, A BETTER TOMORROW established the formal and thematic template for a
  new era of crime movie: everything that has followed\, from Woo's follow
 -up masterpieces THE KILLER and HARD-BOILED to the Triad films of Johnnie
  To to myriad international imitators. ... The advanced techniques of the
  Hong Kong action cinema translated from the period kung fu and wuxia fil
 m to the modern world of cops and robbers\, from swordplay to gunplay....
  Precision cutting and flowing camera movement would come to define the a
 ction film\, blowing away the tension-based American system with a surfei
 t of bullets\, preferably fired by a man clenching a matchstick in his te
 eth\, firing two handguns at once in a vain attempt at exorcising his exi
 stential pain." -- Sean Gilman\, The Chinese Cinema
DTSTAMP:20260923T174022Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261003T160000
LAST-MODIFIED:20260923T174022Z
