UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53982887854935
DTSTART:20261007T160000Z
DTEND:20261007T170000Z
DESCRIPTION:Thomas Day was a freeborn Black artisan renowned for his work i
 n North Carolina and Virginia. Known as an enslaver with ties to northern 
 abolitionists and the American Colonization Society\, Day illustrates the 
 moral complexities of life in pre-Civil War North Carolina. In this presen
 tation\, Assistant Site Manager David Rayburn of Thomas Day State Historic
  Site will discuss Day's contradictory life and the research conducted sin
 ce his home and workshop were acquired by the State of North Carolina in 2
 024.
GEO:36.296532;-76.219757
LOCATION:Museum of the Albemarle\, Gaither Auditorium
SUMMARY:History for Lunch: Thomas Day – Cabinetmaker and Free Person of C
 olor
URL;VALUE=URI:https://events.dncr.nc.gov/event/history-for-lunch-thomas-day
 -cabinetmaker-and-free-person-of-color
CATEGORIES:Education Programs
CATEGORIES:Lectures & Talks
END:VEVENT
