END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Calendar Group African American Cultural Center": "https://calendar.ncsu.edu/event/equip-fall-wellness-workshop"}
X-SOURCE-ID:calendar_group_african_american_cultural_center
X-SOURCE:NC State African American Cultural Center
DTSTAMP:20261003T050348Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53374549856476
DTSTART:20261022T213000Z
DTEND:20261022T233000Z
DESCRIPTION:Are you ready to thrive at NC State? If so\, join the African A
 merican Cultural Center (AACC) for the EQUIP Fall Wellness Workshop. EQUIP
  is a year-long initiative that will support first-year students as they c
 ultivate self-awareness\, resilience and community. Grounded in social-emo
 tional learning\, EQUIP's psychoeducational workshops empower first-year s
 tudents to cultivate self-awareness\, personal agency and emotional intell
 igence as they navigate college rigor. This interactive session will cover
  common first-year challenges\, including academic stress\, identity devel
 opment and help-seeking habits. Through structured reflection\, scenario e
 xploration and peer modeling\, participants will build vital skills in res
 ilience and proactive resource engagement to thrive inside and outside the
  classroom.\n\nIf you have questions about EQUIP or the session\, contact 
 Andaiye Qaasim\, assistant director of AACC\, at aaqaasim@ncsu.edu.
GEO:35.785108;-78.674529
LOCATION:Witherspoon Student Center\, African American Cultural Center (Roo
 m 356)
SUMMARY:EQUIP Fall Wellness Workshop
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.ncsu.edu/event/equip-fall-wellness-workshop
CATEGORIES:Workshops and Training
CATEGORIES:Student Life
CATEGORIES:High-Impact Experiences
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events 1 Ics": "https://events.dncr.nc.gov/event/ghostwalk-new-bern"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_1_ics
X-SOURCE:NC Cultural Resources
DTSTAMP:20261003T050337Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53046594450003
DTSTART:20261022T220000Z
DTEND:20261023T013000Z
DESCRIPTION:Join us for Ghostwalk Oct. 22-24\, 2026 from 6pm to 9pm. Now in
  its 36th year\, Ghostwalk is a family-friendly fall event that combines s
 pirits from New Bern's past\, talented local volunteer actors\, historic s
 ites\, and a fun-filled\, creative environment. For three evenings in Octo
 ber you’ll be able to step into the time machine and visit with ghosts o
 f lively New Bernians\, famous people and ordinary folks. You’ll visit 1
 3 “ghost sites” in Cedar Grove Cemetery and on the porches of historic
  homes. In keeping with this year’s theme\, “Voices of Freedom\, Stori
 es from 250 Years”\, you will hear stories and meet characters from all 
 across our 250 year history.
GEO:39.944168;-75.165017
LOCATION:Historic Downtown New Bern
SUMMARY:Ghostwalk New Bern
URL;VALUE=URI:https://events.dncr.nc.gov/event/ghostwalk-new-bern
CATEGORIES:Performances & Arts
CATEGORIES:Theater & Musicals
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events 1 Ics": "https://events.dncr.nc.gov/event/mecklenburgs-declaration-ncs-revolutionary-presbyterians"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_1_ics
X-SOURCE:NC Cultural Resources
DTSTAMP:20261003T050337Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53178468608553
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261023
DESCRIPTION:An article published in the April 30\, 1819 edition of the Rale
 igh Register and North Carolina Gazette proclaimed the following: “It is
  not probably known to many of our readers\, that the citizens of Mecklenb
 urg County\, in this State\, made a Declaration of Independence more than 
 a year before Congress made theirs. The following Document on the subject 
 has lately come to the hands of the Editor from unquestionable authority\,
   and is published that it may go down to posterity.”  \n\nThe document 
 in question was called the Mecklenburg Declaration of Independence\, and i
 t had allegedly declared Mecklenburg “free and independent” from Briti
 sh rule on May 20\, 1775. Of the 27 men who were said to have signed the d
 ocument\, almost all were Presbyterian.\n\nBut many people were confused 
 – why were they just now hearing about this revolutionary action? The or
 iginal document burned in a house fire in 1800\, and the published version
  had been reconstructed from notes made by the clerk of the 1775 meeting. 
 No extant copies could be found.\n\nThe debate over the authenticity of th
 e Mecklenburg Declaration began. And\, when a copy of a document called th
 e Mecklenburg Resolves written on May 30\, 1775\, was discovered in a 1775
  newspaper\, the debate intensified. The Resolves were revolutionary\, but
  stopped short of declaring independence. Did both documents exist? Or was
  the Declaration being conflated with the Resolves?\n\nExamine the evidenc
 e and decide for yourself by visiting the new exhibit\, “Mecklenburg Dec
 laration?” at the Presbyterian Heritage Center.\n\nThe PHC is open Tuesd
 ay-Friday (10-4) and Saturday (12-4).
GEO:35.646737;-82.300463
LOCATION:Presbyterian Heritage Center
SUMMARY:Mecklenburg's Declaration: NC's Revolutionary Presbyterians
URL;VALUE=URI:https://events.dncr.nc.gov/event/mecklenburgs-declaration-ncs
 -revolutionary-presbyterians
CATEGORIES:Exhibits & Tours
CATEGORIES:Exhibitions
END:VEVENT
